=begin pod

=head1 LLM::Chat

Simple framework for LLM inferencing in Raku. Supports multiple backends
(OpenAI-compatible, KoboldCpp), chat templates (ChatML, Llama 3/4, Mistral,
Gemma 2, and any HuggingFace Jinja2 template), conversation management
with context shifting, and token counting.

=head2 Synopsis

=begin code :lang<raku>
use LLM::Chat::Backend::KoboldCpp;
use LLM::Chat::Template::ChatML;
use LLM::Chat::Conversation;

my $backend = LLM::Chat::Backend::KoboldCpp.new(
    api_url  => 'http://localhost:5001/v1',
    template => LLM::Chat::Template::ChatML.new,
);

my $conv = LLM::Chat::Conversation.new;
$conv.add-message('user', 'Hello!');

my $response = $backend.text-completion($conv.messages);
=end code

=head2 Templates

=head3 Built-in Templates

=begin code :lang<raku>
use LLM::Chat::Template::ChatML;
use LLM::Chat::Template::Llama3;
use LLM::Chat::Template::Llama4;
use LLM::Chat::Template::MistralV7;
use LLM::Chat::Template::Gemma2;
use LLM::Chat::Template::DeepSeekV4;

my $template = LLM::Chat::Template::ChatML.new;
=end code

=head3 DeepSeek V4

DeepSeek V4 publishes a Python message encoder instead of a Jinja chat
template. C<LLM::Chat::Template::DeepSeekV4> implements the prompt-encoding
half of that protocol (not completion parsing):

=begin code :lang<raku>
use LLM::Chat::Template::DeepSeekV4;

my $template = LLM::Chat::Template::DeepSeekV4.new;
my $prompt = $template.render(@messages);

# Full upstream message shape, retaining JSON member order:
my $prompt = $template.encode-json(
    $messages-json,
    thinking-mode    => 'thinking',
    reasoning-effort => 'high',
);
=end code

C<encode-messages> accepts associative structures and supports context,
thinking removal, tools, DSML calls/results, response formats, developer and
latest-reminder roles, and quick tasks. C<encode-json> is preferable when the
exact JSON wire-member order must be reflected in the rendered prompt.

=head3 Jinja2 Templates (HuggingFace)

Load any HuggingFace C<chat_template> directly from a C<tokenizer_config.json>:

=begin code :lang<raku>
use LLM::Chat::Template::Jinja2;

# From tokenizer_config.json
my $json = 'tokenizer_config.json'.IO.slurp;
my $template = LLM::Chat::Template::Jinja2.from-tokenizer-config($json);

# Or provide the template string directly
my $template = LLM::Chat::Template::Jinja2.new(
    template  => $jinja2-string,
    bos-token => '<s>',
    eos-token => '</s>',
);
=end code

The Jinja2 template support is powered by
L<Template::Jinja2|https://raku.land/zef:apogee/Template::Jinja2>,
a complete Jinja2 engine for Raku with byte-identical output to Python Jinja2.

=head2 Backends

=head3 KoboldCpp

=begin code :lang<raku>
use LLM::Chat::Backend::KoboldCpp;

my $backend = LLM::Chat::Backend::KoboldCpp.new(
    api_url   => 'http://localhost:5001/v1',
    template  => $template,  # for text completions
    max_tokens => 200,
);
=end code

KoboldCpp reports a generation that ended on one of your C<stop>
strings as C<finish_reason: "length"> — the same as one that ran out of
budget. When a completion finishes with C<length>, this backend asks
KoboldCpp's C<GET /api/extra/perf> why it stopped, and finishes it as
C<stop> when that was a stop string and the token counts match (or,
with no usage to match, are well under C<max_tokens>). The wire value
stays readable:

=begin code :lang<raku>
$resp.finish-reason;            # 'stop'
$resp.provider-finish-reason;   # 'length'
$resp.finish-reason-evidence;   # { source, verdict, note, stop_reason, last_token_count, ... }
=end code

Anything short of a clear answer leaves C<length>. The check is
bounded (C<verify-length-finish-timeout>, 2 seconds) and can be turned
off with C«verify-length-finish => False». The rules and their
limits are in
L<LLM::Chat::Backend::KoboldCpp|../lib/LLM/Chat/Backend/KoboldCpp.rakumod>.

=head3 OpenAI-compatible

Any OpenAI-compatible API (vLLM, Ollama, etc.):

=begin code :lang<raku>
use LLM::Chat::Backend::OpenAICommon;

my $backend = LLM::Chat::Backend::OpenAICommon.new(
    api_url => 'http://localhost:8000/v1',
    model   => 'my-model',
);
=end code

=head3 Message normalization

Some chat templates (Mistral's, among others) refuse any conversation
that is not an optional system message followed by strictly
alternating user and assistant turns — and llama.cpp or KoboldCpp
C<--jinja> run the model's own template. Every OpenAI-compatible
backend can reshape the outgoing conversation to fit:

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $backend = LLM::Chat::Backend::KoboldCpp.new(
    api_url               => 'http://localhost:5001/v1',
    message-normalization => 'alternate-roles',   # default 'none'
);
=end code

Adjacent plain messages of one role merge (contents joined with a
blank line), the leading system messages become one, and a system
message later in the conversation becomes user text under a
C<[System message]> marker line, merged into the user turn beside it.
Tool calls and tool results are never merged or crossed; a user
message straight after tool results, which such a template reads as
the user speaking twice, gets an assistant turn in front of it saying
C<tool-result-acknowledgement> (C<'Understood.'>; C<''> for none).
Nothing is reordered, and the result is a pure function of the
messages — a
repeated conversation prefix normalizes to the same bytes, so prompt
caches keep matching. It applies to the chat paths' C<messages> and to
the messages the text paths render through their template. The full
rules are in
L<LLM::Chat::Backend::OpenAICommon|../lib/LLM/Chat/Backend/OpenAICommon.rakumod>.

=head3 Structured output and field order

Set C<json_schema> on the settings to constrain the reply's shape. It
goes out as C<response_format> on OpenAI-compatible backends and as
the native C<json_schema> field on KoboldCpp. Servers that compile the
schema into a grammar (llama.cpp, KoboldCpp) make the model write an
object's fields in the order the schema lists them, and LLM::Chat
lists a schema's C<properties> in the order of its C<required> array,
then any optional properties alphabetically — so B<the order of
C<required> is the order the model writes the fields>:

=begin code :lang<raku>
my $settings = LLM::Chat::Backend::Settings.new(
    json_schema => %(
        type       => 'object',
        properties => %(
            change   => %( type => 'string' ),
            evidence => %( type => 'string' ),
        ),
        required   => [ 'evidence', 'change' ],   # evidence first
    ),
);
=end code

Every request body is serialized deterministically — the same request
is the same bytes on every call and in every process, which also keeps
provider prompt caches warm. The full rule, including nested schemas,
C<$defs> and tool parameters, is in
L<LLM::Chat::WireJSON|../lib/LLM/Chat/WireJSON.rakumod>, which is
exported for building prompt text with the same guarantees.

=head3 Mock (for tests)

Canned-response backend for unit and integration tests. Returns
pre-configured responses in order, records every call for assertions,
and can be scripted to fail on specific calls to exercise retry /
fallback paths in downstream consumers.

=begin code :lang<raku>
use LLM::Chat::Backend::Mock;
use LLM::Chat::Backend::Settings;

my $mock = LLM::Chat::Backend::Mock.new(
    settings  => LLM::Chat::Backend::Settings.new,
    responses => ['first', 'second', 'third'],
    # Optional: script per-call failures by index. Returning a defined
    # hash fails that call; returning Nil proceeds normally.
    error-producer => -> $i {
        when $i == 0 { { class => 'http', status => 503,
                         message => 'bad gateway' } }
        default      { Nil }
    },
    # Optional: script a truncated-but-successful call by index. The
    # call still succeeds with its canned body; the Response just
    # carries finish-reason 'length', the way a real blocking
    # completion reports an exhausted token budget.
    finish-reason-producer => -> $i { $i == 0 ?? 'length' !! Str },
);

my $resp = $mock.chat-completion(@messages);
# $mock.recorded-calls[0]<messages>, <response>, <error>, <call-index>, ...
# $mock.call-index — monotonic counter, bumped on every call
=end code

See L<LLM::Chat::Backend::Mock|../lib/LLM/Chat/Backend/Mock.rakumod>
for the full attribute list and recording contract.

=head2 Response

Every completion method returns an C<LLM::Chat::Backend::Response>
(or C<::Stream> for streaming calls). Callers poll C<.is-done>, read
C<.msg> on success, and inspect C<.err> on failure.

Responses also carry structured error metadata on the failure path
so consumers can classify errors without regex-parsing raw messages:

=begin code :lang<raku>

until $resp.is-done { sleep 0.01 }

if $resp.is-success {
    say $resp.msg;
}
else {
    say "failed: {$resp.err}";
    say "  class:  {$resp.error-class  // '(none)'}";   # 'http' / 'timeout' /
                                                        # 'connection' /
                                                        # 'response' / 'unknown'
    say "  status: {$resp.error-status // '(none)'}";   # HTTP code when
                                                        # error-class eq 'http'
}

=end code

C<error-class> values:

=item C<'http'> — HTTP-level error. C<error-status> is populated with the code.
=item C<'timeout'> — request exceeded the client-side deadline.
=item C<'connection'> — network unreachable / connection reset / DNS failure.
=item C<'response'> — HTTP succeeded but the body was malformed, empty, or finished with a C<'length'> / C<'content_filter'> quit.
=item C<'unknown'> — catch-all for exceptions that don't classify.

L<LLM::Data::Inference::Task|https://raku.land/zef:apogee/LLM::Data::Inference>
reads these fields to decide between abort / retry-same / advance in
its model-fallback policy — consumers that want the same policy
without depending on that module can implement it against the
C<Response.error-class> / C<.error-status> pair directly.

Provider-reported usage is also available on the Response when the
backend emits it:

=begin code :lang<raku>

$resp.prompt-tokens;       # Int, undefined on backends that don't emit usage
$resp.completion-tokens;   # Int
$resp.total-tokens;        # Int
$resp.cached-prompt-tokens;# Int, the cached SLICE of prompt-tokens; undefined
                           # when the provider says nothing about caching
                           # (which is not the same as nothing cached)
$resp.cost;                # Num (credits)
$resp.model-used;          # Str, provider-reported routed model
$resp.provider-id;         # Str, provider-assigned request id
$resp.finish-reason;       # Str ('stop' / 'length' / 'content_filter' / ...)
$resp.provider-finish-reason; # Str, the reason exactly as the wire said it —
                           # differs only when a backend corrected it
$resp.finish-reason-evidence; # Hash, how a backend decided, when it checked

=end code

=head3 How a stream ends

A streamed generation ends in one of three ways, and every backend
built on C<LLM::Chat::Backend::OpenAICommon> settles the Response the
same way for each:

=item B<The provider says it is finished> — C<[DONE]>, or a C<stop> /
C<tool_calls> finish reason and then C<[DONE]> or a closed body.
C<.is-done> and C<.is-success> are both true.
=item B<The provider names a terminal failure> — C<length>,
C<content_filter>, or a reason this library doesn't know.
C<.is-success> is false, C<.error-class> is C<'response'>, and
C<.finish-reason> says which. Note that C<length> is a failure: a
generation stopped by the completion budget is a truncated one, and it
never arrives labelled as a complete answer. The partial text is still
on C<.latest> / C<.msg>, and the usage is on the token counters when
the provider sent it.
=item B<The body just stops> — no C<[DONE]>, no finish reason. A
dropped connection, a killed upstream worker, a proxy that gave up.
C<.is-success> is false with C<.error-class> C<'response'>; there is
no way to tell a reply that was cut off from one that ended.

A finish reason is recorded when it arrives but acted on only when the
stream ends, because the usage frame (C<stream_options.include_usage>)
comes I<after> the finish chunk — a stream settled on the finish chunk
never reported its token counts. Content after the finish chunk is
ignored; a server that sends its finish chunk and then goes quiet is
settled after C<stream-finish-grace> seconds (default 10) and its
connection dropped.

A streamed C<data:> line that doesn't parse is skipped rather than
fatal, but counted on C<.dropped-frames>:

=begin code :lang<raku>

$resp.dropped-frames;      # Int, 0 unless frames were unparseable

=end code

If any frames were dropped B<while tool calls were being assembled>,
the stream fails whatever else happened — tool-call C<arguments> are
streamed as JSON fragments concatenated blind, so a hole in them can
produce valid JSON saying something the model never asked for, and
nothing downstream could detect it. Prose-only streams keep their
success and leave the counter for the caller to judge.

=head3 Cancelling a generation

There is a fourth way a generation can end that isn't the provider's
decision: the caller changes its mind. C<cancel> is for that, and it
stops the B<server> as well as the client.

=begin code :lang<raku>

my $resp = $backend.chat-completion-stream(@messages);
# ... the user hits escape, or a newer request supersedes this one ...
$backend.cancel($resp);      # or $resp.cancel — same effect

$resp.is-done;               # True, immediately and synchronously
$resp.is-cancelled;          # True
$resp.err;                   # 'Cancelled by user'
$resp.error-class;           # undefined — a cancel is not a failure
$resp.latest;                # whatever had already arrived, kept

=end code

The OpenAI-compatible protocol has no abort message: a client that no
longer wants a completion hangs up, and llama.cpp's server, OpenRouter
and the gateways between them stop generating when it does. So C<cancel>
closes the HTTP connection carrying the generation. Without that it
would only quit the local supply — the caller sees nothing more while
the model generates, and bills for, the entire reply anyway. On a slow
model with a caller that cancels freely, that accumulates abandoned
generations nobody will ever read.

Three properties are worth relying on:

=item B<The local close is synchronous and unconditional.> The supply is
quit before any network work, so a hung or unreachable endpoint cannot
delay or prevent the cancel. Tearing down the connection is best-effort
and swallows its own errors — safe to call straight from a keybind
handler.
=item B<A cancel during prefill is not lost.> Before response headers
arrive there is nothing to close, and Cro's client offers no handle on a
request in flight. The Response remembers that a cancel was asked for
and aborts the instant the transport exists, so the abort lands on the
first response byte rather than never. Prefill is exactly when a caller
gets bored, so this is the common case.
=item B<A cancel after the generation finished is a no-op.> A late or
duplicate cancel changes nothing; a completed Response keeps
C<.is-success>.

A cancelled Response is deliberately B<not> classified —
C<.error-class> stays undefined — so a fallback policy reading the
Response cannot mistake an abandoned request for a flaky backend and
retry it.

C<LLM::Chat::Backend::KoboldCpp> additionally sends
C<POST /api/extra/abort>, which stops a generation still in prefill,
before there is a connection to close. Its C<cancel> returns the
background C<Promise> for that POST; it is fire-and-forget and callers
may sink it.

=head2 Tool Loop

C<LLM::Chat::ToolLoop> wraps a streaming backend and runs OpenAI-style
tool-call rounds until the model produces a final answer or the safety
limits are reached. It is backend-agnostic: pass tools in OpenAI format
and an executor callback that returns C<role => "tool"> result hashes.

=begin code :lang<raku>

use LLM::Chat::ToolLoop;

my @tools = $mcp-server.tools-for-llm;

my $loop = LLM::Chat::ToolLoop.new(
    backend => $backend,
    tools => @tools,
    execute-tools => -> @calls {
        $mcp-server.execute-tool-calls(@calls)
    },
    # Defaults: 4 tool rounds, 12 total calls, 2 identical calls.
);

my $resp = $loop.chat-completion-stream(@messages);
until $resp.is-done { sleep 0.01 }
say $resp.msg if $resp.is-success;

=end code

For KoboldCpp, use the OpenAI-compatible chat endpoint
C<http://host:5001/v1>. Streaming tool calls arrive as
C<delta.tool_calls> chunks and are exposed through C<.tool-calls> on
the response before the loop executes them.

=head2 Retry Policy

C<LLM::Chat::Retry> is the shared retry/fallback policy: five pure subs
that classify a failure, decide how long to wait, sleep without
ignoring a cancel, and build the attempt/telemetry records. Pair it
with C<LLM::Chat::Retry::Exceptions> for the typed failures a chain
throws when it gives up.

=begin code :lang<raku>

use LLM::Chat::Retry;
use LLM::Chat::Retry::Exceptions;

my @attempts;
my Int $retries-left = 2;

given classify-error(
    error-class  => $resp.error-class,
    error-status => $resp.error-status,
) {
    when 'abort'      { die "config/account error: {$resp.err}" }
    when 'retry-same' {
        my Num $wait = retry-backoff(3 - $retries-left);   # 1s, 2s, 4s ...
        $retries-left--;
        # Returns False the moment &cancelled flips — a cancelled run
        # never sits out a 16-second backoff.
        last unless sleep-with-cancel($wait, :&cancelled);
    }
    default { }   # 'advance' — try the next backend at once
}

@attempts.push: attempt-record(
    backend-index => 0, model => $backend.model, error => "{$resp.err}",
);

X::LLM::Chat::Retry::Exhausted.new(
    :@attempts, summary => 'all backends exhausted',
).throw;

=end code

Buckets: C<'abort'> (HTTP 400/401/402/403/404 — config, account or
access errors that repeat identically), C<'retry-same'> (5xx,
connection drops, anything unclassifiable — usually one upstream
provider having a bad minute), C<'advance'> (timeout, 429, malformed
response, parser failure — model-specific pathologies).

The exception types are C<Exhausted>, its subclasses C<Truncated>
(cut off by C<max_tokens>) and C<TimedOut> (missed a deadline), and
C<Cancelled> — which is deliberately B<not> an C<Exhausted>, so a user
pressing Ctrl-C never lands in a dead-letter queue. C<Truncated>
declares C<item-retryable> C<False>, the duck-typed advice an
orchestration layer reads as C<< $ex.?item-retryable // True >> before
re-running failed work.

L<LLM::Data::Inference::Task|https://raku.land/zef:apogee/LLM::Data::Inference>
runs on this module, and its own exception types subclass these — so
handlers written against either hierarchy match.

=head2 Conversation Management

=begin code :lang<raku>
use LLM::Chat::Conversation;

my $conv = LLM::Chat::Conversation.new;
$conv.add-message('system', 'You are helpful.');
$conv.add-message('user', 'Hello!');
$conv.add-message('assistant', 'Hi there!');

# Access messages
say $conv.messages;
=end code

=head2 Token Counting

=begin code :lang<raku>
use LLM::Chat::TokenCounter;

my $counter = LLM::Chat::TokenCounter.new(
    tokenizer => $tokenizer,
    template  => $template,
);

my $conversation-tokens = $counter.get-conversation-count(@messages);

# Exact selected-model request: [context head, messages, context tail],
# including the tool catalogue rendered by the model template.
my $request-tokens = $counter.get-request-count(
    @messages,
    :@tools,
    context-head => $run-context.head-message,
    context-tail => $run-context.tail-message,
);
=end code

C<get-conversation-count> remains useful for stored-conversation and
compaction accounting. Use C<get-request-count> for a preflight that must
match the complete wire prompt for one selected model. Undefined context
ends and an empty tool catalogue are omitted without changing template bytes.

=head2 Dependencies

=item L<Cro::HTTP> — HTTP client for API calls
=item L<Template::Jinja2|https://raku.land/zef:apogee/Template::Jinja2> — Jinja2 template engine
=item L<Tokenizers> — HuggingFace tokenizers via Rust FFI
=item L<JSON::Fast> — JSON parsing

